✅✅ 2-Phase Commit (2PC) ✅✅

Used for distributed transactions where multiple services/databases must either:

Commit together
OR
Rollback together

Example:
Payment Service
Inventory Service
Accounting Service

A coordinator manages the transaction.

Phases
1. Prepare Phase
Coordinator asks all services:
"Can you commit?"

Services:
validate transaction
acquire locks
respond YES/NO

2. Commit Phase
If all respond YES:
Coordinator -> COMMIT

Otherwise:
Coordinator -> ROLLBACK

Problems with 2PC
1. Slowest Service Controls Transaction
Overall latency depends on the slowest participant.

2. Long-Held Locks
Locks remain until all services finish.
This reduces concurrency and hurts scalability.

3. Coordinator Failure Problem
If coordinator dies:
participants remain blocked
locks may never release
services don't know whether to commit or rollback


✅✅ Saga Pattern ✅✅

SAGA is a sequence of local transactions.
If one fails, compensating transactions
undo the previous steps.

Saga avoids distributed locking and 2PC.

Instead of one global transaction:
each service commits independently
failures trigger compensating actions

Works on:
Eventual Consistency

Example
Normal Flow
Payment Service -> charge card
Inventory Service -> reserve stock
Accounting Service -> create entry

Compensation Flow
If inventory fails:

Payment Service -> refund
Accounting -> reverse entry

These are:
Business-level rollbacks

Saga Types
1. Choreography
Services communicate using events.
No central controller.

2. Orchestration
Central saga orchestrator controls flow.

Orchestrator Failure vs 2PC Coordinator Failure
Saga orchestrator usually stores progress in DB/message log.

If it crashes:
Restart -> continue from saved state

Unlike 2PC:
no global locks remain stuck
services are not blocked waiting forever

Problems with Sagas
Compensation is Messy
Undoing business actions is difficult.

Examples:
emails/notifications cannot be undone
third-party APIs may not support rollback
refunds must be idempotent

System may temporarily remain inconsistent while compensations run.
User sees charge before refund completes